<Base title="Editorial Standards" description="How Drink Memphis Beer reports, sources, scores and corrects: our rules for accuracy, independence, conflicts of interest, AI use and contributor work." pageType="Article">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <div class="label">How we work</div>
    <h1 class="text-7xl mt-1">Editorial standards</h1>
    <p class="text-xl mt-4">Drink Memphis Beer is independent. These are the rules every page follows, whether Will wrote it or a contributor did.</p>

    <div class="prose-dmb mt-8">
      <h2>Accuracy and sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Every factual page lists its sources at the bottom: brewery websites, news reports, public records, and our own visits.</li>
        <li>Brewery status (open, closed, changed hands) carries the month we last checked it. The <a href="/status/">Status Board</a> shows every one.</li>
        <li>When sources disagree, say on a beer's ABV, we say so and leave the number off rather than guess.</li>
        <li>Quotes are exact. Anything said off the record stays off the record.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Scores</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The Pour Score (one to five pints, or "Tap Water") and the Tap Water Test verdict are set by Will Finney alone, so every score means the same thing.</li>
        <li>A beer without a score says so. We never fill in a score to make a page look complete.</li>
        <li>Will has a go-to brewery (Wiseacre) and says so on his <a href="/about/">about page</a>. Every beer still has to pass the same test.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No brewery, bar or distributor pays for coverage or for a score.</li>
        <li>Advertising and sponsorships are labeled as such and kept apart from editorial pages. See the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a>.</li>
        <li>Free beer or event tickets don't buy coverage. If something we wrote about was provided free, the page says so.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Conflicts of interest</h2>
      <p>Writers disclose any tie to a business they cover: a job, ownership, family, a sponsorship, a paid gig. A writer with a tie doesn't review that business, and if their story mentions it, the page discloses the connection.</p>

      <h2>Contributors</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Contributor stories carry the writer's byline and link to their author page. Reporting help on a brewery or beer page is credited at the bottom of that page.</li>
        <li>Every piece is edited and fact-checked before it runs, and the writer sees the final version first.</li>
        <li>Want to contribute? See <a href="/write-for-us/">write for us</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>AI and images</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We use software, including AI tools, to help research, organize and maintain the site: checking listings, building pages from our data, drafting summaries that an editor then checks against sources.</li>
        <li>Opinions, scores and first-person reporting come from people. Contributors tell us if they used AI for anything beyond spelling and grammar.</li>
        <li>Cartoon scenes featuring Will are illustrations made from real photos, and the <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a> explains how. Photos of breweries and beer are real unless labeled otherwise.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>Found a mistake? <a href="/contact/">Tell us</a>. We fix errors quickly. Every page shows its last-updated date, and a correction that changes the meaning of a story is noted on that story.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
